============================================================================
[C3] middle class f = R^{-p}(log R)^q against (star_1):  full algebra
============================================================================
  bookkeeping: file
    p=1    q=0       LHS/RHS = 2*R**2/(A*C)
    p=0    q=0       LHS/RHS = 0
    p=-1   q=0       LHS/RHS = 0
    p=-1   q=1       LHS/RHS = 1/(A*C*log(R)**2)
    p=-1   q=-1      LHS/RHS = (2 - log(R))/(A*C*log(R))
    p=2    q=0.5     LHS/RHS = R**3*(-0.25*log(R)**0.5 - 2.5*log(R)**1.5 + 6*log(R)**2.5)/(A*C*log(R)**3.0)
  bookkeeping: consistent
    p=1    q=0       LHS/RHS = 2*R/(A*C)
    p=0    q=0       LHS/RHS = 0
    p=-1   q=0       LHS/RHS = 0
    p=-1   q=1       LHS/RHS = 1/(A*C*R*log(R)**2)
    p=-1   q=-1      LHS/RHS = (2 - log(R))/(A*C*R*log(R))
    p=2    q=0.5     LHS/RHS = R**2*(-0.25*log(R)**0.5 - 2.5*log(R)**1.5 + 6*log(R)**2.5)/(A*C*log(R)**3.0)

  --- exponent matching in general (log-power bookkeeping), file's (star_1) ---
  f'' ~ R^{-p-2}[ p(p+1)L^q - q(2p+1)L^{q-1} + q(q-1)L^{q-2} ]
  RHS ~ C R^{-2p-3} L^{2q}
  (i)  R-powers equal : -p-2 = -2p-3  =>  p = -1
  (ii) with p=-1 the L^q term has coefficient p(p+1)=0, so LHS log-degrees = {q-1, q-2},
       RHS log-degree = 2q.  Need 2q=q-1 AND 2q=q-2  =>  contradiction for every real q.
  (iii) coefficient check at p=-1: f=R, f''=0 => LHS=0 while RHS=C g2N R^2/R^3 = C g2N/R != 0.
  => even p=-1 (the file's 'only exponent') is NOT a solution: LHS vanishes identically.

  --- same test, dimensionally consistent bookkeeping c1 f''/R = C f^2/R^3 ---
  (i)  R-powers equal : -p-3 = -2p-3  =>  p = 0
  (ii) then f = A L^q, and LHS log-degrees {q, q-1, q-2}, RHS 2q  => again contradiction
       (and for p=0 exactly, the p(p+1) term vanishes identically: LHS = q R^{-3}(...) vs RHS R^{-3})
  => under BOTH bookkeepings: no solution in the whole two-parameter power-log family.

  --- area law check (file's sec.5 item 2) ---
  f = e^{-sigma R^2}:  LHS/RHS (file) = 2*R**3*sigma*(2*R**2*sigma - 1)*exp(R**2*sigma)/C
  -> ratio = e^{+sigma R^2}*(4 sigma^2 R^2-2 sigma)*R^3/C  -> +infinity: the area law fails (star_1) too.
  NOTE: for the area law the bilinear factor is NOT f^2 -- the two arcs' minimal surfaces add,
        W(C_xy)W(C_yx) = W(C) = f.  So 'f^2' is class-dependent (right for power, wrong for area).

============================================================================
[C1] the dictionary, written out
============================================================================
  Let kappa = log(entropy of surfaces of unit area)  [a combinatorial constant of the 4D lattice,
  independent of beta and NOT a functional of c_n].  Let sigma_0 = lim_n (-log c_n)/n and
  alpha the sub-exponential exponent of c_n ~ e^{-kappa n} n^{alpha-1}.
     (E) W ~ e^{-sigma R^2}                <=> sigma_0 > kappa          (a GAP in the ratio test)
     (P) W ~ R^{-p}                        <=> sigma_0 = kappa exactly, and c_n ~ e^{-kappa n} n^{-p/2-1}
     (I) W ~ R^{-p}(log R)^q               <=> sigma_0 = kappa exactly, and c_n ~ e^{-kappa n} n^{-p/2-1}(log n)^q
  Derivation of the exponent map: W(R) = sum_{A <= A_max(R)} Omega_A prod_p c_{j_p}; with Omega_A ~ e^{kappa A}
  and c ~ e^{-kappa A} A^{alpha-1}, the summand is A^{alpha-1}, and A_max ~ R^2, so W ~ R^{2 alpha}.
  Matching R^{-p} = R^{2 alpha} gives alpha = -p/2, i.e. c_n ~ e^{-kappa n} n^{-p/2-1}.  q passes through as-is.
  What fixes (P) vs (I) is alpha and q per se, NOT kappa; but the (E) vs (P),(I) border is sigma_0 vs kappa,
  and kappa is NOT computable from c_n.
